Summary: winemac: HostWindows option; a native host application shows the windows
Category: graphics
Kind: feature
Feature: embedding
Origin: wine-mac-builds
  Note: the OpenGL part grew out of the IOSurface probe of 2026-11-17
Upstream: no
Why: An app that embeds Wine wants a program's windows inside its own
 windows, scaled or letterboxed as it likes, and the input back, without
 the program putting windows of its own on screen. The protocol is
 docs/app-embedded-protocol.md, its wire format
 docs/app-embedded-protocol.h (copied here as wmh_protocol.h, which is
 offered under LGPL-2.1-or-later OR MIT, as hosts compile it in). With
 HostWindows ("y"; HKCU\Software\Wine\Mac Driver, or per program under
 AppDefaults\<exe>\Mac Driver) and WINEMAC_HOST_SERVICE naming a host's
 Mach service, the driver does the handshake before Cocoa starts. If it
 succeeds, each window's Cocoa functions send messages to the host instead
 of making an NSWindow, window surfaces are drawn into IOSurfaces the host
 is sent (up to three per window, each handed back by surface_release),
 and a receive thread turns the host's input, focus, close and quit
 events into the usual macdrv events. Without the variable, or if the host
 doesn't answer in 10 seconds, the plain driver is used and an err: line
 says why. NSApplication still runs (for the main-thread dispatch, event
 queues, displays, the pasteboard and keyboard layouts), but the process
 shows no window, Dock icon or menu bar of its own. The design doc said
 no NSApplication; keeping it changed much less of the driver.
 OpenGL (and Direct3D through wined3d, and DirectDraw wrappers that use
 OpenGL) goes to the host as views: each client surface is a view of its
 window, and framebuffer 0 of its drawable is remapped, in the GL functions
 the driver hands out, to an FBO on an IOSurface from the view's pool;
 each present finishes, sends the surface (bottom-up) and points the FBO
 at a free one. Win32u, opengl32 and wined3d are unchanged. The context
 is kept current with no view attached. The cursor goes to the host too:
 a system cursor by its NSCursor name, an image as an IOSurface (the
 first frame of an animated one), hidden; SetCursorPos and ClipCursor are
 passed on, and the cursor position is the last one the host reported.
 The host can ask for a window's size limits (WM_GETMINMAXINFO) and say
 a resize is starting; the driver posts those as queries without waiting
 and answers with window_min_max. During a live resize the host asks
 about each new size (QUERY_RESIZE_SIZE, with the edge being dragged);
 the driver sends the program WM_SIZING, as the plain driver does, and
 answers with resize_size_reply. Repeated identical messages aren't
 resent.
 If the host's connection fails (it goes away, or a receive fails), the
 program is asked to quit.
 Covered: the handshake, windows, GDI surfaces, OpenGL views, cursor,
 size limits, WM_SIZING, keys, mouse, focus, close and quit; displays_get is
 winemac-host-displays.
Programs: Second Chance (an app that embeds Wine): shows a program inside
 its own window
Enable: reg HKCU\Software\Wine\Mac Driver\HostWindows = y (REG_SZ) (or per program: AppDefaults\<exe>\Mac Driver)
 env WINEMAC_HOST_SERVICE=<the host's Mach service name> (needed as well; inherited by child processes)
Messages: "HostWindows is on but WINEMAC_HOST_SERVICE isn't set; using
 the plain driver", "host: no host named \"%s\" (%s); using the plain
 driver" and the other "host: ...; using the plain driver" lines, "host:
 the host has gone away; asking the program to quit", "host: receive
 failed: %s (%x)" (err:macdrv; WINEDEBUG=err-macdrv or -all hides them)
Risk: opens a Mach connection to the bootstrap service WINEMAC_HOST_SERVICE
 names, sends it the program's window pictures and cursor, and takes the
 program's input, focus, close and quit from it. A process of the same
 user that holds that name gets them (it already runs as the user). It's
 on when the prefix has HostWindows (which any program in the prefix can
 write) and the environment Wine starts in names the service.
Known issues: Metal and Vulkan aren't covered: no Metal view is attached,
 so programs drawing through DXVK, vkd3d, D3DMetal or Vulkan show nothing
 in the host. Not done yet: animated cursors (the first frame is sent),
 window shape and alpha, and window_get_frame as a
 call (the driver keeps the frame it last set or the host last reported).
 Windows of other processes as owners aren't supported (protocol v1). The
 GL remap covers glBindFramebuffer(EXT), glDrawBuffer(s), glReadBuffer and
 glGetIntegerv; a program that asks about framebuffer 0 another way
 (glGetBooleanv, glGetFloatv, glGetInteger64v, glGetIntegeri_v) sees the
 FBO. A destroyed window's FBO is deleted only in the context current when
 it's destroyed; other contexts' stay until those contexts go, and a
 drawable used by more than 8 contexts forgets the oldest one's (logged).
Uncertain: what waiting for glFinish at each present costs a demanding
 program; d3d9test drew 279 frames in 18.3 s hosted, 19.9 s on the plain
 driver.
Docs: docs/app-embedded-protocol.md
Expects before it: winemac-centre-fullscreen-gdi,
 winemac-export-macdrv-functions (context), winemac-plugins (context),
 winemac-activation-policy (context); centre-fullscreen-gdi for the surface
 offset it extends
Related: winemac-clip-follows-window, winemac-cocoa-start-wait; cursor
 clips go through macdrv_clip_cursor, which host mode hooks, and host mode
 still waits for Cocoa to start
Status: 11.22: 2026-11-28: QUERY_RESIZE_SIZE (WM_SIZING during a live
 resize) added; host quick build quick20261128T010948 (meeka, M1 Max,
 macOS 26.7.2): wmh-testhost's 100x80 bottom-right question to a window
